## Work Style

Think before coding. Read the named source of truth, understand the current
repo shape, and surface real uncertainty instead of guessing. Proceed with
safe, reversible, local work when the task is clear; ask only for destructive,
credential-gated, external-production, or materially branching decisions.

Keep the implementation simple. Prefer deletion, reuse, and local patterns over
new abstractions. Do not add speculative features, compatibility layers, or
dependencies unless the task explicitly requires them.

Make surgical changes. Touch only the files needed for the request, preserve
project-local content, and do not reformat or rewrite unrelated guidance.

Work toward verified goals. Identify what proves the change, run the focused
checks, read the results, and report the evidence honestly.

## Kkachi Boundaries

- KAS owns skill/process/prompt policy, task contracts, templates, and evidence
  expectations. It does not own deterministic project state or backend runtime
  control.
- KAH owns deterministic artifacts, schemas, events, locks, diagnostics, and
  mechanical gates. It does not own policy prose or subjective language
  judgment.
- KAB owns backend bridge/session/control and retained execution evidence. It
  does not own KAS task policy and must not provide hidden fallback execution.

Do not mutate profiles, auth, tokens, secrets, provider/model/gateway settings,
KAH source/state, KAB runtime/policy, commits, pushes, or deployment unless the
current task explicitly authorizes that exact operation.

## English Compact Output

KAS-generated product output must be English by default. Keep console/chat
reports compact and artifact-first:

- Status
- Summary
- Files
- Verification
- Risks/blockers
- Detailed artifact
- Next action requested

Write long reasoning, plans, diffs, logs, and reviews to the requested artifact
path when the task requires detail. If the artifact cannot be written, report a
blocked status instead of pasting raw detail into chat.

